@Petrol

Cultural differences, again! When thinking about a sandwich, what I'm visualizing in my head is our Polish kanapka. Of course, if I were to take a kanapka with me somewhere, I'd put it between two pieces of bread, purely for practical reasons (that's what my wife packs for her lunch at work). But most sandwiches eaten at home in Poland are just one piece of bread with stuff on top: I grew up eating single slices of bread with a generous spread of butter and a single slice of ham, or cheese, occasionally with a lettuce lead, a slice of tomato, or a few slices of cucumber.

More bread means more empty calories, and usually one piece of it is enough to provide that extra bit of flavor and texture to go with whatever is the star of the thing (ham, maybe, or jam, or whatever it is you enjoy).

@Andrzej
The only problem with one slice of bread for your sandwich is the mess that's created when all of that little stuff starts falling off the bread. At least with two slices, the top one can hold the other components down. In my never-ending dieting days, I'd keep pushing the top piece further away, still holding the stuff down, but never eat it. Same with burger buns.

Often, when I'd eat a sandwich on a roll or a bagel, I'd have them scoop out the bread inside. Not only does it same on carbs, but it makes a nice well to hold things like tuna salad.

@Andrzej
That combo of nuts and roasted pepper sounds fantastic!  I am a huge fan of almond butter--that's what I spread on bread for breakfast (along with Nocciolat, an Italian improvement on Nutella)--but I'd love to find Muhammara. May try that recipe, though for lunch not breakfast.
Interesting how some people ( cultures) go for sweet for breakfast and others hate the very idea. On a tour of China, we mostly stayed in large hotels whose breakfasts catered to tourists of many nationalities (Marmite, anyone?) so we tried most of them. Spouse got quite fond of noodle soup with greens; me..okay occasionally I guess.

